A CSRF token is a secret the server issues that only *your own pages* can read. A cross-site attacker can cause a request but cannot read your DOM or your response bodies, so they cannot supply it.

The common **double-submit cookie** pattern, and the frontend half of it:

```js
// Server sets a readable token cookie alongside the HttpOnly session cookie:
//   Set-Cookie: csrfToken=<random>; SameSite=Lax; Secure   (no HttpOnly — JS must read it)

function csrfToken() {
  return document.cookie
    .split('; ')
    .find((c) => c.startsWith('csrfToken='))
    ?.split('=')[1] ?? '';
}

await fetch('/api/transfer', {
  method: 'POST',
  credentials: 'include',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    'X-CSRF-Token': csrfToken(),   // server compares header against the cookie
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ to, amount }),
});
```

The server rejects the request unless the header matches the cookie. The attacker's page can make the browser *send* the cookie but has no way to *read* it, so it cannot set a matching header.

The stronger variant is the **synchronizer token**: the server stores a per-session token and compares against that, rather than trusting the cookie to be its own witness. Prefer it when a subdomain you don't fully control could set cookies on your domain.
